1786890192404	{"id":100,"item":{"type":"events","session_id":"f1d00a21-1929-4c85-a0ad-cf4fbb6bed96","epoch":117105630862556080,"records":[{"seq":81,"ts_ms":1786890192401,"event":{"kind":"provider","event":{"kind":"assistant_text","text":"Both files are done — I read `notes.md` and wrote `summary.md` with a one-paragraph summary. Here's the detailed implementation explanation.\n\n# Implementing a Virtualized Transcript View in Rust with gpui\n\nThe core problem described in the notes — every row rendered every frame, full remeasure on each streaming delta, scroll jumps when messages settle — is the classic failure mode of treating a transcript as a single monolithic element tree. The fix is to make the transcript a *windowed* view over an incrementally-maintained row model, where measurement, layout, and paint are all scoped to what's actually visible and what actually changed.\n\n## Architecture overview\n\nThe design splits into three layers:\n\n- **Row model** — a flat `Vec<Row>` (or a rope/sum-tree for very long transcripts) where each row has a stable `RowId`, a content fingerprint, and a cached measured height.\n- **Height index** — a prefix-sum structure (gpui's `sum_tree` crate is ideal) mapping scroll offset ↔ row index in O(log n), so we can answer \"which rows intersect the viewport?\" without touching every row.\n- **Viewport renderer** — a gpui `Element` implementation (or `uniform_list`/custom `list` usage) that materializes only the visible rows plus a small overscan margin, keyed by `RowId` so gpui can reuse element state across frames.\n\ngpui already ships a `list` element with lazy measurement, but for a streaming transcript with heterogeneous heights (markdown, code fences, tool cards) you generally want your own `ListState`-style controller so you control invalidation granularity.\n\n## The row model\n\nStable identity is what prevents the \"code fence reflows when it closes\" problem from cascading. Each row gets an ID at creation that never changes, and a fingerprint that changes only when its *content* changes:\n\n```rust\nuse std::sync::Arc;\n\n#[derive(Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Debug)]\npub struct RowId(u64);\n\n#[derive(Clone)]\npub struct Row {\n    pub id: RowId,\n    /// Hash of the semantic content (text, fence language, role).\n    /// Changes here invalidate measurement; unchanged rows are never remeasured.\n    pub fingerprint: u64,\n    pub content: RowContent,\n    /// Cached height at the last measured width; None = needs measure.\n    pub measured: Option<MeasuredHeight>,\n}\n\n#[derive(Copy, Clone)]\npub struct MeasuredHeight {\n    pub width: gpui::Pixels,\n    pub height: gpui::Pixels,\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone)]\npub enum RowContent {\n    Markdown(Arc<str>),\n    CodeFence { lang: Arc<str>, text: Arc<str>, closed: bool },\n    ToolCall { title: Arc<str>, collapsed: bool },\n}\n```\n\nWhen a streaming delta arrives, you don't rebuild the vector. You apply a **splice**: locate the affected row (almost always the last one), update its content and fingerprint, and mark only that row's `measured` slot dirty. The height index gets a point update, not a rebuild. This is the \"splice-based updates\" idea from the notes made concrete — the edit surface is O(changed rows), not O(all rows).\n\nA subtlety worth encoding: while a code fence is *open* (still streaming), render it with a reserved minimum height and defer syntax-highlight layout until it closes. That way the close event changes the fingerprint once, triggers one remeasure, and you don't reflow on every token inside the fence.\n\n## Measurement and the height index\n\nVirtualization lives or dies on knowing heights without laying everything out. The strategy:\n\n1. **Estimate first.** New rows enter the index with an estimated height (e.g. line-count × line-height for text, fixed chrome + lines for fences).\n2. **Measure lazily.** When a row scrolls into the viewport (plus overscan), measure it for real at the current width and write back `MeasuredHeight`.\n3. **Reconcile deltas.** When a real measurement replaces an estimate, the height index shifts everything below it — this is where scroll anchoring (next section) matters.\n4. **Invalidate by width.** On window resize, don't drop all measurements; keep them tagged with the width they were measured at and remeasure only rows entering the viewport whose cached width differs.\n\nThe index itself can be gpui's `SumTree` with a summary of `{count: usize, height: Pixels}`:\n\n```rust\npub struct HeightIndex {\n    tree: sum_tree::SumTree<RowEntry>,\n}\n\nimpl HeightIndex {\n    /// Rows whose vertical extent intersects [top, bottom), plus overscan.\n    pub fn visible_range(&self, top: Pixels, bottom: Pixels, overscan: Pixels) -> Range<usize> {\n        let mut cursor = self.tree.cursor::<HeightSummary>(&());\n        cursor.seek(&(top - overscan), Bias::Left, &());\n        let start = cursor.start().count;\n        cursor.seek(&(bottom + overscan), Bias::Right, &());\n        let end = cursor.start().count + 1;\n        start..end.min(self.tree.summary().count)\n    }\n\n    /// Point-update one row's height; O(log n), returns the delta applied.\n    pub fn set_height(&mut self, index: usize, height: Pixels) -> Pixels { /* splice one entry */ }\n}\n```\n\n## Scroll anchoring (\"anchor-flip\")\n\nThe scroll-jump problem happens because scroll position is stored as an absolute pixel offset, and heights above the viewport change (estimates → real measurements, streaming rows growing). The fix is to store scroll position as an **anchor**: a `(RowId, pixel_offset_within_row)` pair, plus a mode flag:\n\n```rust\npub enum ScrollAnchor {\n    /// Pinned to the bottom: follow streaming output.\n    Bottom,\n    /// Pinned to a specific row; height changes elsewhere don't move it.\n    Row { id: RowId, offset_in_row: Pixels },\n}\n\nimpl TranscriptView {\n    fn resolve_scroll_top(&self, viewport: Pixels) -> Pixels {\n        match self.anchor {\n            ScrollAnchor::Bottom => self.heights.total() - viewport,\n            ScrollAnchor::Row { id, offset_in_row } => {\n                self.heights.row_top(self.index_of(id)) + offset_in_row\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe \"flip\" logic: when the user scrolls up from the bottom, flip from `Bottom` to `Row` anchored at the topmost visible row; when they scroll back within a small threshold of the end, flip back to `Bottom`. When a streaming message settles and its final measurement lands, rows *above* the anchor may change height — you recompute `resolve_scroll_top` from the anchor rather than preserving a stale pixel offset, so the content the user is reading stays put. This single change eliminates the settle-jump described in the notes.\n\n## Paint-only fade for incoming text\n\nFading in new tokens must not trigger layout. Implement it in the paint phase: stamp each appended text run with a frame counter (gpui provides frame timing via the window; avoid re-requesting layout), and during `paint`, compute opacity from elapsed frames and draw the run with that alpha. Request only *repaint* (`cx.notify()` on an animation tick) while the fade is active — layout and measurement are untouched, so the fade costs one quad's worth of paint work, not a reflow.\n\n## Invalidation granularity summary\n\n| Event | Row model work | Height index work | Layout/measure work | Paint work |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Streaming delta (text) | Update 1 row + fingerprint | 1 point update (if estimate grows) | Measure 1 row if visible | Visible rows only |\n| Code fence closes | Update 1 row + fingerprint | 1 point update | 1 remeasure (highlight now) | Visible rows only |\n| New message row | Push 1 row | 1 insert (estimated) | Measure if visible | Visible rows only |\n| Window resize | None | Lazy per-row on entry | Visible rows at new width | Visible rows only |\n| Fade animation tick | None | None | **None** | Alpha change only |\n| User scrolls | None | 2 seeks (O(log n)) | Newly entered rows | Visible rows only |\n\n## Implementation order\n\n1. Introduce `RowId`, fingerprints, and the flat row model behind the existing renderer, verifying IDs are stable across streaming updates.\n2. Build the `HeightIndex` with estimated heights and wire `visible_range` into a custom element that only constructs visible children.\n3. Add lazy real measurement with write-back, tagged by width.\n4. Replace absolute scroll offset with `ScrollAnchor` and implement the bottom/row flip.\n5. Move the fade to paint-only, and defer code-fence highlighting until fence close.\n6. Profile with a long transcript under streaming load; the target is frame cost proportional to viewport size, independent of transcript length.\n\nThe end state directly answers each observed problem: fingerprints kill the full remeasure, deferred fence layout kills mid-stream reflow, and anchor-based scrolling kills the settle jump — while gpui's retained element identity (keyed by `RowId`) lets the framework skip diffing everything that didn't change."}}}]}}
