1786890646781	{"id":100,"item":{"type":"events","session_id":"f1d00a21-1929-4c85-a0ad-cf4fbb6bed96","epoch":117105630862556080,"records":[{"seq":163,"ts_ms":1786890646780,"event":{"kind":"provider","event":{"kind":"assistant_text","text":"# Writing a Tiny Markdown Parser in Rust — A Walkthrough\n\nLet's build a deliberately small parser that handles headings, paragraphs, code fences, and inline emphasis. The goal isn't CommonMark compliance — that's a *much* deeper rabbit hole — but a clean two-phase design you could grow into one: **block parsing first, inline parsing second**. This mirrors how real implementations like `pulldown-cmark` are structured, because block structure (is this line inside a fence?) must be resolved before inline structure (is this `*` emphasis or a literal asterisk?) can even be asked.\n\n## Step 1: Model the output\n\nStart with the AST. Keeping it small forces clarity about what we actually support:\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]\npub enum Block {\n    Heading { level: u8, inlines: Vec<Inline> },\n    Paragraph(Vec<Inline>),\n    CodeFence { lang: String, text: String },\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]\npub enum Inline {\n    Text(String),\n    Code(String),          // `inline code`\n    Emphasis(Vec<Inline>), // *italic*\n    Strong(Vec<Inline>),   // **bold**\n}\n```\n\nNote that `Emphasis` and `Strong` contain `Vec<Inline>`, not `String` — emphasis nests (`**bold with *italic* inside**`), so the inline grammar is recursive while the block grammar is flat. That asymmetry is the single most important design fact about markdown.\n\n## Step 2: The block pass\n\nThe block parser walks the input **line by line**, and its only state is *whether we're inside a code fence*. Everything else is decidable from the current line alone:\n\n```rust\npub fn parse_blocks(input: &str) -> Vec<Block> {\n    let mut blocks = Vec::new();\n    let mut lines = input.lines().peekable();\n\n    while let Some(line) = lines.next() {\n        if line.trim().is_empty() {\n            continue; // blank lines just separate blocks\n        }\n\n        // Code fence: consume until the closing ```\n        if let Some(rest) = line.strip_prefix(\"```\") {\n            let lang = rest.trim().to_string();\n            let mut text = String::new();\n            for body in lines.by_ref() {\n                if body.trim_end() == \"```\" {\n                    break;\n                }\n                text.push_str(body);\n                text.push('\\n');\n            }\n            blocks.push(Block::CodeFence { lang, text });\n            continue;\n        }\n\n        // ATX heading: 1-6 leading #'s followed by a space\n        let hashes = line.bytes().take_while(|&b| b == b'#').count();\n        if (1..=6).contains(&hashes) && line.as_bytes().get(hashes) == Some(&b' ') {\n            let inlines = parse_inlines(&line[hashes + 1..]);\n            blocks.push(Block::Heading { level: hashes as u8, inlines });\n            continue;\n        }\n\n        // Paragraph: greedily absorb continuation lines\n        let mut para = line.to_string();\n        while let Some(next) = lines.peek() {\n            let stop = next.trim().is_empty()\n                || next.starts_with(\"```\")\n                || next.starts_with('#');\n            if stop {\n                break;\n            }\n            para.push(' ');\n            para.push_str(lines.next().unwrap());\n        }\n        blocks.push(Block::Paragraph(parse_inlines(&para)));\n    }\n    blocks\n}\n```\n\nTwo things worth noticing here:\n\n- The fence loop uses `lines.by_ref()` so the inner `for` borrows the same iterator — when the fence closes, the outer loop resumes *after* it. No index juggling.\n- The paragraph branch has to know what *ends* a paragraph, which means it duplicates a little knowledge of the other block starts:\n  - blank line — the universal separator\n  - a fence opener — otherwise ` ``` ` mid-paragraph would be swallowed as text\n  - a heading marker — headings interrupt paragraphs in markdown; this is a real spec rule, not a shortcut\n\nThis duplication is the classic block-parser annoyance. Real implementations centralize it into a \"can this line start a new block?\" predicate; at our size, inlining it is fine.\n\n## Step 3: The inline pass\n\nInlines are where markdown gets genuinely tricky, because delimiters are **contextual** — a `*` might open emphasis, close it, or mean nothing. Our tiny version uses a recursive scanner over bytes with a lookahead for closing delimiters:\n\n```rust\nfn parse_inlines(src: &str) -> Vec<Inline> {\n    let mut out = Vec::new();\n    let mut text = String::new();\n    let bytes = src.as_bytes();\n    let mut i = 0;\n\n    // Helper: flush accumulated plain text before pushing a structured node\n    macro_rules! flush {\n        () => {\n            if !text.is_empty() {\n                out.push(Inline::Text(std::mem::take(&mut text)));\n            }\n        };\n    }\n\n    while i < bytes.len() {\n        match bytes[i] {\n            b'`' => {\n                if let Some(end) = src[i + 1..].find('`') {\n                    flush!();\n                    out.push(Inline::Code(src[i + 1..i + 1 + end].to_string()));\n                    i += end + 2;\n                } else {\n                    text.push('`'); // unmatched backtick is literal\n                    i += 1;\n                }\n            }\n            b'*' => {\n                let strong = bytes.get(i + 1) == Some(&b'*');\n                let (delim, dlen) = if strong { (\"**\", 2) } else { (\"*\", 1) };\n                if let Some(end) = src[i + dlen..].find(delim) {\n                    flush!();\n                    let inner = parse_inlines(&src[i + dlen..i + dlen + end]);\n                    out.push(if strong {\n                        Inline::Strong(inner)\n                    } else {\n                        Inline::Emphasis(inner)\n                    });\n                    i += end + 2 * dlen;\n                } else {\n                    text.push('*');\n                    i += 1;\n                }\n            }\n            _ => {\n                // Push the full UTF-8 char, not just the byte\n                let ch = src[i..].chars().next().unwrap();\n                text.push(ch);\n                i += ch.len_utf8();\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    flush!();\n    out\n}\n```\n\nThe recursion in the `*` branch is what makes `**bold with *italic***` work: we slice out the delimited span and re-run the same parser on it. The unmatched-delimiter fallback (`text.push('*')`) is essential — without it, a lone asterisk in prose would either crash the parser or eat the rest of the line.\n\nOne deliberate bug to be aware of: greedy `find` means `*a* and *b*` parses correctly, but `**a* b**` resolves in ways CommonMark's delimiter-stack algorithm would handle differently. That's the tradeoff of a 60-line inline parser, and it's fine to accept it *knowingly*.\n\n## Step 4: Rendering — proving the AST is usable\n\nA parser without a consumer is untestable in practice. A minimal HTML renderer doubles as an integration test:\n\n```rust\npub fn to_html(blocks: &[Block]) -> String {\n    fn inlines(out: &mut String, nodes: &[Inline]) {\n        for node in nodes {\n            match node {\n                Inline::Text(t) => out.push_str(&escape(t)),\n                Inline::Code(c) => {\n                    out.push_str(\"<code>\");\n                    out.push_str(&escape(c));\n                    out.push_str(\"</code>\");\n                }\n                Inline::Emphasis(inner) => {\n                    out.push_str(\"<em>\");\n                    inlines(out, inner);\n                    out.push_str(\"</em>\");\n                }\n                Inline::Strong(inner) => {\n                    out.push_str(\"<strong>\");\n                    inlines(out, inner);\n                    out.push_str(\"</strong>\");\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    let mut html = String::new();\n    for block in blocks {\n        match block {\n            Block::Heading { level, inlines: nodes } => {\n                html.push_str(&format!(\"<h{level}>\"));\n                inlines(&mut html, nodes);\n                html.push_str(&format!(\"</h{level}>\\n\"));\n            }\n            Block::Paragraph(nodes) => {\n                html.push_str(\"<p>\");\n                inlines(&mut html, nodes);\n                html.push_str(\"</p>\\n\");\n            }\n            Block::CodeFence { lang, text } => {\n                let class = if lang.is_empty() {\n                    String::new()\n                } else {\n                    format!(\" class=\\\"language-{lang}\\\"\")\n                };\n                html.push_str(&format!(\"<pre><code{class}>{}</code></pre>\\n\", escape(text)));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    html\n}\n\nfn escape(s: &str) -> String {\n    s.replace('&', \"&amp;\").replace('<', \"&lt;\").replace('>', \"&gt;\")\n}\n```\n\nEscaping happens **at render time, once, at the leaves** — never inside the parser. If you escape during parsing, round-tripping the AST back to markdown becomes impossible and you'll double-escape the moment two code paths both \"helpfully\" sanitize.\n\n## Step 5: Tests that earn their keep\n\nTest the weird cases, not the happy path — the happy path was already exercised while writing the code:\n\n```rust\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn nested_emphasis() {\n        let ast = parse_blocks(\"**bold *and italic***\");\n        assert_eq!(\n            ast,\n            vec![Block::Paragraph(vec![Inline::Strong(vec![\n                Inline::Text(\"bold \".into()),\n                Inline::Emphasis(vec![Inline::Text(\"and italic\".into())]),\n            ])])]\n        );\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn unclosed_fence_consumes_to_eof() {\n        let ast = parse_blocks(\"```rust\\nlet x = 1;\\n\");\n        assert!(matches!(&ast[0], Block::CodeFence { text, .. } if text.contains(\"let x\")));\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn lone_asterisk_is_literal() {\n        let ast = parse_blocks(\"a * b\");\n        assert_eq!(ast, vec![Block::Paragraph(vec![Inline::Text(\"a * b\".into())])]);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe unclosed-fence test encodes a *policy decision*: we chose \"consume to end of file\" (matching CommonMark) rather than erroring. Whatever you choose, a test pins it so the behavior is intentional rather than accidental.\n\n## Where to go from here\n\n- Blocks to add next, roughly in order of difficulty:\n  - Blockquotes — introduce *container blocks*, which change the game:\n    - every line must be stripped of its `> ` prefix before the inner content is parsed\n    - containers nest, so your line-by-line loop becomes a recursive descent over prefixes\n  - Lists — the hardest block feature by far:\n    - tight vs. loose list detection depends on blank lines *between* items\n    - continuation lines depend on indentation relative to the marker, not the margin\n- Inline features worth adding:\n  - Links `[text](url)` — needs bracket matching with nesting\n  - Escapes `\\*` — a one-line fix in the scanner, and the first thing users will hit\n\n## Comparing parsing strategies\n\n| Strategy | How it works | Strengths | Weaknesses | Best when |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Hand-rolled two-phase** (this walkthrough) | Line-based block pass, then recursive inline scan per block | Full control, zero dependencies, easy to debug, matches markdown's real two-level grammar | Delimiter edge cases pile up; you slowly reinvent the CommonMark spec | Learning, constrained subsets, embedded/no-dep contexts |\n| **Parser combinators** (`nom`, `winnow`) | Compose small parsers (`tag`, `take_until`, `alt`) into a grammar | Declarative, reusable pieces, great error positions | Markdown's context-sensitivity (setext headings, lazy continuation, emphasis precedence) fights the combinator model hard | Formats with a *real* grammar — config languages, protocols; awkward for markdown itself |\n| **Event/pull parser** (`pulldown-cmark` style) | Emit a stream of `Start(Tag)`/`Text`/`End(Tag)` events; no AST unless the consumer builds one | Near-zero allocation, streams huge documents, spec-compliant implementations exist to study | Consumers must track state across events; harder to transform documents than with a tree | Production rendering pipelines, large inputs, when you'd otherwise just call `pulldown-cmark` anyway |\n\nThe honest conclusion: for anything user-facing, use `pulldown-cmark` and spend your effort elsewhere. But building the tiny version is the fastest way to understand *why* the event-stream design won — markdown's grammar is two loosely-coupled layers, and the hand-rolled version makes you feel exactly where the coupling leaks."}}}]}}
